title: 'World ID live test: Selfie Check access-gated; fixed action + key normalization'
summary: 'Live-tested VERIFICATION_MODE=world with the user''s real World ID creds. The RP signing key was initially the wrong value (122-char base64 / 91 bytes); once the correct 32-byte hex private key was set, signRequest + /verify-context produced a valid RP signature (app_1148..., rp_58b0...). The selfie itself fails with a generic client-side error because the World ID ''selfie'' credential is a gated beta the user has now requested access to. Refactored to the idiomatic World ID pattern: one fixed action (WORLD_ACTION, default agentic-commerce-purchase, now registered in the portal) bound per-purchase via the widget signal; added 0x/whitespace key normalization and clearer widget error surfacing.'
symbols_touched:
  - '#worldid-service'
  - '#verify-route'
  - '#storefront-verify-page'
  - ^personhood-verified
files_modified:
  - server/src/services/worldid.ts
  - server/src/routes/verify.ts
  - server/.env.example
  - client/src/VerifyPage.tsx
learnings:
  - 'World ID RP signing key must be a 32-byte hex string (64 hex chars, 0x optional). The Developer Portal issues a keypair; use the PRIVATE key. A wrong/base64 value throws ''Invalid signing key: contains non-hex characters'' in signRequest — the async error handler caught it as a clean 500 (no hang), validating that fix in the wild.'
  - 'The ''selfie'' (Selfie Check) credential is a gated beta: request access for the app from the World team (docs point to developers@toolsforhumanity.com). Without access the IDKit widget fails client-side with a generic error before any proof reaches the backend (order stays pending_verification).'
  - 'Idiomatic World ID: ONE fixed action per flow (register in the portal''s Actions section), bind the specific request via the preset `signal` (we use the session id) — not a new action per request. Refactored verificationAction() to a fixed WORLD_ACTION (default agentic-commerce-purchase).'
  - app_id/rp_id are public and safe to log for debugging; the signing key is secret and is normalized (trim + strip 0x) before signRequest.
verification:
